    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so basically the business environment itself becomes a proxy for the demographic profile of the area. if we use these indicators, doesn't that essentially mean we're penalizing applicants in historically underserved or diverse neighborhoods just because their surroundings don't look like \"traditional\" high-growth suburbs? how do you justify using those specific local indicators as legitimate risk metrics if they end up just filtering out certain racial or socioeconomic groups?",
